---
import EditorialArticleLayout from '../../layouts/EditorialArticleLayout.astro';
import { whatsappIntelligenceUrl } from '../../data/intelligence';

const title = 'Pockets, Skorts and Hidden Storage: What Reddit Shoppers Want From Functional Activewear';
const seoTitle = 'Activewear Pockets and Skort Design Guide | FITCLO';
const description =
  'A private label manufacturing guide to leggings with pockets, tennis skorts with pockets, running shorts phone pockets, waistband storage and utility activewear design.';
const articleImage = '/images/product-categories/gym-shorts-hero.png';

const whatsappUrl = new URL(whatsappIntelligenceUrl);
whatsappUrl.searchParams.set(
  'text',
  'Hello FITCLO, I want to source activewear with useful pockets, skorts or phone storage. Please recommend ready-stock and custom options.',
);

const quickTakeaways = [
  'Pockets are no longer a small add-on. Shoppers want phone storage, key security and less bounce during running, tennis, gym and travel.',
  'The best pocket position depends on the product: leggings need stable side pockets, running shorts often need waistband or liner storage, and skorts need hidden inner-short pockets.',
  'A pocket that fits a phone on a flat sample may still bounce, sag or distort once the wearer moves.',
  'Private label buyers should specify phone size, pocket depth, closure type and movement testing before approving bulk production.',
  'Utility details can create stronger product pages because buyers search for very specific phrases such as tennis skort with pockets and running shorts with phone pocket.',
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Are pockets important for activewear sales?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Pockets answer a practical shopper problem: where to place a phone, key, card, gel or earbuds case during movement. They also create clear search keywords for product pages.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What pocket is best for running shorts?',
    answer:
      'A rear waistband pocket or close-fitting liner thigh pocket usually controls bounce better than a loose outer-shell pocket. The exact choice depends on fit, waistband stability and phone size.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should tennis skorts include pockets?',
    answer:
      'For many buyers, yes. Inner short pockets can hold tennis balls, phones or cards while keeping the outer skirt clean and flattering.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can FITCLO customize pocket placement?',
    answer:
      'Yes. FITCLO can help develop side pockets, hidden waistband storage, inner-short pockets, zipper pockets and private label utility details.',
  },
];

const relatedReading = [
  {
    title: 'Activewear Design Trends 2026',
    link: '/blogs/activewear-design-trends-2026',
    desc: 'How bold colors, seamless knits and performance utility are shaping activewear demand.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Capri Leggings Are Back',
    link: '/blogs/capri-leggings-workout-set-trend-2026',
    desc: 'Why cropped workout sets and 3/4 leggings are returning in 2026.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Studio-to-Street Luxury Trends in Activewear',
    link: '/blogs/studio-to-street-luxury-trends',
    desc: 'How everyday wearability affects activewear product design.',
  },
];
---

<EditorialArticleLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  type="REDDIT TREND GUIDE"
  articleKind="trend-guide"
  date="July 23, 2026"
  readTime="9 min read"
  articleImage={articleImage}
  quickAnswerTitle="Functional activewear is winning because shoppers want attractive products that also solve daily problems: carrying a phone, securing keys, storing tennis balls, keeping gels organized and avoiding pocket bounce. For brands, pocket design should be engineered into the garment from the first sample, not added at the end."
  quickTakeaways={quickTakeaways}
  faqs={faqs}
  relatedReading={relatedReading}
  ctaTitle="Build Functional Activewear with Useful Storage"
  ctaText="Share your target product category and storage requirement. FITCLO can help compare side pockets, waistband pockets, skort pockets and custom utility construction."
  ctaHref={whatsappUrl.toString()}
  ctaLabel="Ask About Pocket Activewear"
  publishedIso="2026-07-23"
  modifiedIso="2026-07-23"
>
  <figure class="fitclo-editorial-feature-image">
    <img
      src={articleImage}
      alt="Functional activewear shorts and skorts with pocket design planning"
      width="1800"
      height="1000"
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <figcaption>The pocket has become a performance feature: it must hold the object securely while the garment keeps its fit and silhouette.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What Reddit and Runner Communities Reveal</h2>
  <p>
    Discussions around women's running shorts, leggings and skorts often return to the same frustration: pockets exist, but many do not actually hold what shoppers carry. Reddit-summary tools such as
    <a href="https://gummysearch.com/tools/best-products/running-shorts/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">GummySearch's running shorts review analysis</a>
    show that communities including r/running and r/XXRunning repeatedly talk about shorts that stay put, do not ride up and include storage that works in motion.
  </p>
  <p>
    Larger product reviews point in the same direction. Nike notes that phone pocket size, secure closure and overall running performance matter together, while
    <a href="https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/topics/clothing-womens/best-running-shorts-womens?n=0" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">OutdoorGearLab's women's running shorts testing</a>
    evaluates pocket depth, bounce control and whether a phone can fit with a case. These are exactly the details activewear buyers should specify to a factory.
  </p>

  <h2>The New Search Language Around Utility Activewear</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Keyword cluster</th>
        <th>Buyer intent</th>
        <th>Best product response</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Leggings with pockets</strong></td>
        <td>Phone storage for errands, gym and walking.</td>
        <td>Deep side pockets with stable opening tension.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Running shorts with phone pocket</strong></td>
        <td>Secure phone carry without bounce.</td>
        <td>Rear waistband pocket, liner thigh pocket or zip-secured pocket.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Tennis skort with pockets</strong></td>
        <td>Ball, phone or key storage with a clean skirt look.</td>
        <td>Inner-short pocket, ball pocket and optional side slit construction.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Workout shorts with zipper pocket</strong></td>
        <td>Security for keys, card and small valuables.</td>
        <td>Low-bulk zipper pocket placed away from high-friction zones.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A Pocket Is a Fit System</h2>
  <p>
    A pocket changes the garment. It adds fabric layers, weight, stitching and tension. If the pocket is placed on a weak waistband, the phone may drag the garment down. If it is placed too low on the thigh, the phone can bounce or tap during running. If the opening is too loose, the item can slide out. If it is too tight, the wearer may struggle to access it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The solution is to develop the pocket around a real object. For a running phone pocket, define the benchmark phone and case. For a tennis skort, define whether the pocket is for a tennis ball, phone or card. For leggings, define whether the pocket should be invisible, oversized or deliberately sport-utility styled.
  </p>

  <h2>Pocket Placement Comparison</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Placement</th>
        <th>Best for</th>
        <th>Buyer risk</th>
        <th>Sample test</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Side legging pocket</strong></td>
        <td>Phone, card, daily use.</td>
        <td>Pocket bulge or phone slipping during squats.</td>
        <td>Walk, squat, lunge and stair test with phone.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Rear waistband pocket</strong></td>
        <td>Running phone or key storage.</td>
        <td>Bounce, sweat concentration or hard object pressure.</td>
        <td>Jog test, sprint test and sit comfort check.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Liner thigh pocket</strong></td>
        <td>2-in-1 running shorts and skorts.</td>
        <td>Phone tapping the thigh or pulling liner down.</td>
        <td>Run, jump and inner-thigh chafe test.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Inner skort pocket</strong></td>
        <td>Tennis balls, phone, golf card.</td>
        <td>Visible pocket outline under the skirt.</td>
        <td>Serve, bend, sit and walk test.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How Brands Can Use This Trend Without Making the Product Bulky</h2>
  <p>
    The best utility activewear does not look overloaded. A tennis skort can have an inner pocket while the outer layer stays minimal. Leggings can have a clean side pocket with low-profile stitching. Running shorts can use a liner pocket instead of a visible cargo pocket. The goal is function without destroying the silhouette.
  </p>
  <p>
    For SEO and AI search, this also gives each product page more specific language. Instead of only writing "women's gym shorts," a product can target "women's running shorts with phone pocket," "tennis skort with inner shorts and pockets," or "private label leggings with side pockets."
  </p>

  <h2>Factory Questions Before You Approve the Sample</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Which phone size was used to test the pocket?</li>
    <li>Does the pocket need a zipper, foldover opening or elastic edge?</li>
    <li>Will the pocket show through lighter colors?</li>
    <li>Does the loaded pocket pull the waistband down?</li>
    <li>Can the pocket be customized for private label product positioning?</li>
  </ul>
</EditorialArticleLayout>
